The gravitational $N$-body problem has no closed-form solution for $N >= 3$, so its long-term behaviour can only be explored numerically. Over long times, though, the main danger is not the local truncation error of the integrator. It is the slow, systematic violation of the geometric structure of Hamiltonian dynamics: energy drifts, orbits spiral, and phase errors grow quadratically.

This thesis studies this problem systematically. Starting from Newton's law of gravitation, we derive the Hamiltonian formulation, the conservation laws and the exact solution of the two-body problem. We then develop a hierarchy of integrators of increasing sophistication, which runs from the explicit and symplectic Euler methods through Störmer--Verlet (leapfrog) and the classical Runge--Kutta method to Yoshida's fourth- and sixth-order symplectic composition methods and an adaptive Dormand--Prince pair. For each method we derive the order of accuracy, the linear stability limit and whether it preserves the symplectic structure, and we use backward error analysis to explain why symplectic methods keep the energy error bounded.

All methods are implemented in a modular, tested Python package. It uses vectorised force kernels, a common integrator interface with a registry, a reproducible experiment pipeline and an interactive viewer whose overlays show past trails and predicted future trajectories. Nine experiments evaluate the implementation against the theory. The measured convergence orders agree with the theoretical ones to within $0.3$, and symplectic methods show linear instead of quadratic error growth. A Solar System simulation over 1000 years keeps the relative energy error below $3 dot 10^(-6)$ at one force evaluation per simulated day. Close encounters in the Pythagorean three-body problem require adaptive time steps spanning six orders of magnitude. Finally, the energy fluctuation reported in the original course project turns out to be mostly an artefact of an inconsistent energy diagnostic.
